{
  "status": "answered",
  "summary": "The handbook explains that compressor blades are inspected visually (using strong light and rotating the compressor) and with magnetic‑particle or fluorescent‑penetrant methods, and that blending repair is permitted only for minor, rounded damage that stays within manufacturer‑specified limits and shows no cracks.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Compressor blades are inspected by visual examination with a strong light, turning the compressor to check each vane for cracks, and by magnetic‑particle or fluorescent‑penetrant inspection to verify that all damage traces are removed.",
      "citations": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Blending repair may be used for minor, well‑rounded damage to leading or trailing edges when the indentation does not exceed the depth limits set by the engine manufacturer and no cracks are present.",
      "citations": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "The blending process involves stoning out material beyond the damage depth, applying a generous radius at the blend edges, and smoothing the area with a crocus cloth.",
      "citations": [1, 2]
    }
  ],
  "gaps": "",
  "manufacturer_note": "The allowable repair limits and depth values are defined in the engine manufacturer’s service and overhaul instruction manuals (see passages 2 and 3)."
}

